involve teaching you how to build a
distribution of returns analysis and also
an average true range percentage analysis
in Excel,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.000
so that you can then estimate suitable
stops and targets for assets that you're
looking to trade.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.000
And we're going to start off by building a
distribution of returns analysis from
scratch.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:48.000
And then after that, we're going to move
over to using some templates to conduct
the rest of the analysis, which is a much
faster method since a lot of the work is
actually automated.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
And these templates will, of course, be
available to you in the downloads folder
for this video.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:03.000
And we have one spreadsheet template for
distribution of returns or DOR analysis
and one for an average true range
percentage or ATRP analysis.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Now, I'll show you how to use those
templates properly later in the video.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:16.000
And I'm going to do that by using real
examples to calculate potential stops and
targets for individual assets in a similar
way to how Anton did it in the previous
video.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
We're also going to cover a short example
of how you can create stops and targets
for spreads as well.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Now, as you guys know, creating stops and
targets is an important aspect of
preventative risk management.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
And that helps us achieve better
performance as traders.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.000
But it isn't the only preventative risk
management technique available.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:49.000
And I'm going to talk about some others
towards the end of the video, which are
namely setting portfolio limits on things
like gross exposure, cash net exposure and
beta adjusted net exposures.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.000
The reason why we employ all of these PRM
measures is so that we achieve better
trading performance, as I said.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
And there are a number of ways we can
measure our performance, which
incidentally, you will hear me talk about
again in a few videos time.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.000
But the ones I want to mention now are R
score targets and win loss rate targets.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Now, you've heard about R scores before
from previous presentations.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
And this is basically just a ratio of
dollar profits to dollar losses from your
realized P&L.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
And that is your your closed trades in
your portfolio.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.000
A good target to hit for R scores is about
one point five or higher.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
And a great R score is typically around
two or above.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Your win loss rate is fairly
self-explanatory.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:42.000
That is just the percentage of winning
versus losing trades that you make, again,
based on your realized P&L or closed
trades in your account.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.000
And a good win loss ratio is achieving
something like 60 percent profitable
trades and 40 percent loss making trades.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Equally 55-45 as a ratio is OK.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.000
And 65-35 is really, really good over the
long term.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:06.000
So in order to try and hit those targets,
we use PRM techniques like stops and
targets on individual trades.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.000
But also we set various portfolio limits
as well.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:18.000
For example, portfolio gross exposure
limits should be set based on a multiple
of the net cash deposit or margin in our
brokerage accounts.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Now, depending on where you are in the
world and what assets you're trading,
those limits might change.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.000
And I'm going to talk about that more
later.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:33.000
But if you're based in the US trading US
stocks, that limit is legally enforced by
brokers and capped at two times leverage.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:41.000
However, if you're in other parts of the
world and have access to CFD instruments,
then you'll be able to attain much higher
leverage.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:49.000
And so in that case, you need to impose
limits on yourself, which probably should
be around four times leverage.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Anything above that kind of starts to
involve much too much risk.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:02.000
